---
import CalculatorLayout from '@layouts/CalculatorLayout.astro';
import RandomNumberGenerator from '@components/islands/RandomNumberGenerator.astro';
import { getCalculator } from '@data/calculators';
const calc = getCalculator('math', 'random-number-generator')!;
const faqs = [
  { question: 'How random are these numbers?', answer: 'They are generated with the Web Cryptography API, which provides cryptographically strong randomness — far more unpredictable than typical pseudo-random functions. Everything runs in your browser.' },
  { question: 'How do I generate a random number between two values?', answer: 'Set the minimum and maximum to your range and generate — the result is a whole number from the minimum up to and including the maximum. For a number between 1 and 100, enter 1 and 100.' },
  { question: 'Can I generate numbers without repeats?', answer: 'Yes. Tick the "Unique" box and every number in the result will be different. If you request more unique numbers than the range allows, the generator returns as many distinct values as possible.' },
  { question: 'Can I use this to pick lottery numbers?', answer: 'Yes — set the range to your lottery\'s (say 1 to 49), choose how many numbers you need, and tick "Unique" so none repeat. It draws impartially, though of course it cannot improve your odds.' },
  { question: 'What is the difference between true and pseudo-random numbers?', answer: 'Pseudo-random numbers come from a formula and are predictable if you know the starting seed. This generator uses the browser\'s cryptographic source, which draws on unpredictable system entropy — much closer to "true" randomness and suitable where fairness matters.' },
];
---
<CalculatorLayout
  calculator={calc}
  presentation="task-first"
  intro="Generate one or many random numbers within any range, with an option for unique (non-repeating) values — powered by secure browser randomness."
  faqs={faqs}
  reviewMetadata={{ reviewedDate: '2026-08-09' }}
>
  <RandomNumberGenerator />
  <Fragment slot="content">
    <h2>How to use the generator</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Set the <strong>Minimum</strong> and <strong>Maximum</strong> of your range (the defaults are 1 and 100).</li>
      <li>Choose <strong>How many</strong> numbers you want — up to 1,000 at a time.</li>
      <li>Tick <strong>No repeats</strong> if every number must be different, then press <strong>Generate Numbers</strong>.</li>
      <li>Change any setting and the shown result is marked stale; press generate again to apply it.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>What you can use it for</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Drawing raffle or giveaway winners impartially</li>
      <li>Picking lottery-style numbers within a range</li>
      <li>Assigning a random order, sampling, or splitting into groups</li>
      <li>Games, decisions, and settling ties fairly</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>How the randomness works</h2>
    <p>
      The numbers come from your browser's <strong>Web Cryptography API</strong> — the same cryptographic source used for security-sensitive work — rather than the seed-based, repeatable output of a simple pseudo-random function. That makes each draw unpredictable and free of any pattern you could anticipate. For non-repeating draws the generator shuffles the available values with a Fisher-Yates shuffle, which keeps every ordering equally likely.
    </p>

    <h2>An inclusive, whole-number range</h2>
    <p>
      The range is <strong>inclusive at both ends</strong>: a range of 1 to 100 can return 1, can return 100, and anything between. Results are always whole numbers — a fractional minimum is rounded up and a fractional maximum rounded down before drawing — so set the bounds to the exact integers you want in play. For a coin flip use 1 to 2; for a die, 1 to 6.
    </p>

    <h2>Unique vs. repeating draws</h2>
    <p>
      Leave <strong>No repeats</strong> unticked and each number is drawn independently, so the same value can appear more than once — the right behaviour for dice rolls or independent trials. Tick it and every number in the result is distinct. Because you cannot draw more distinct values than the range holds, asking for more unique numbers than the range allows simply returns as many as exist — request 10 unique numbers from 1 to 6 and you get all six.
    </p>

    <h2>A worked example: lottery numbers</h2>
    <p>
      To mimic a 6-from-49 lottery draw, set the minimum to <strong>1</strong>, the maximum to <strong>49</strong>, how many to <strong>6</strong>, and tick <strong>No repeats</strong>. The generator returns six different numbers between 1 and 49, each equally likely — an impartial draw, though of course no generator can improve your odds of matching the real thing.
    </p>

    <h2>Private, and its limits</h2>
    <p>
      Every draw happens on your device and nothing is sent to a server, so your numbers stay entirely yours. Keep in mind that results are integers only and capped at 1,000 per draw, and that true unpredictability is exactly the point — a fresh press can repeat an earlier result by chance. For a strong random password rather than numbers, see the <a href="/everyday/password-generator">password generator</a>; to summarise a set of numbers once you have them, the <a href="/math/statistics-calculator">statistics calculator</a> takes over.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</CalculatorLayout>
